Subject: [PATCH] dkms: honor KERNELVER so the module builds against the target kernel

The lisuangpu-dkms Makefile decided its build kernel from KERNEL_VERSION
(defaulting to `uname -r`), while dkms.conf passes the target kernel as
KERNELVER. Because the two variable names differ, DKMS's requested kernel was
silently ignored: the module was always built against the kernel that merely
happened to be running at build time, yet filed under the *target* kernel's
module tree.

On a machine where a newer kernel is installed while an older one is still
running (the normal `apt upgrade` case), this produces a module with the
wrong vermagic / symbol CRCs. It then refuses to load, e.g.:

    gf: disagrees about version of symbol drm_crtc_vblank_on
    gf: Unknown symbol kmalloc_large (err -2)

and the GPU ends up with no kernel driver bound (black screen / no display).

Fix: honor KERNELVER (the DKMS convention) and fall back to `uname -r` only
for a plain manual `make`. This is a one-line, behavior-preserving change for
manual builds and makes every future `dkms autoinstall` on a kernel upgrade
build against the correct headers.

Apply from the DKMS source root (/usr/src/lisuangpu-dkms-<version>/):
    patch -p1 < 0001-dkms-makefile-honor-KERNELVER.patch
or:
    git apply 0001-dkms-makefile-honor-KERNELVER.patch
---
 Makefile | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/Makefile
+++ b/Makefile
@@ -1,7 +1,7 @@
 DRM_DRIVER_NAME := gf
 
 ifndef KERNEL_VERSION
-KERNEL_VERSION := $(shell uname -r)
+KERNEL_VERSION := $(if $(KERNELVER),$(KERNELVER),$(shell uname -r))
 endif
 LINUXDIR ?= /lib/modules/$(KERNEL_VERSION)/build
 KERNEL_GF_DIR := $(shell \
